Description: Provides distribution-robust estimation and statistical inference
    for bivariate correlations and standardized mean differences (effect sizes)
    without requiring distributional assumptions. Traditional robust methods
    often achieve stability by trimming, Winsorizing, or converting data to
    ranks, thereby inadvertently changing the target estimand and discarding
    authentic tail variance. The methods in this package map empirical data to
    standard normal scores via rank-based inverse-normal transformation (Normal
    Quantile Transformation), model the empirical quantile functions using
    regularized monotone polynomials, and extract distributional moments in
    exact closed algebraic form using monic Probabilists' Hermite polynomials
    and Isserlis' (1918) <doi:10.1093/biomet/12.1-2.134> theorem. For bivariate
    data, the joint covariance is computed analytically under a latent Gaussian
    copula via Mehler's (1866) <doi:10.1515/crll.1866.66.161> bilinear
    expansion. The package provides: (1) The Hermite-Mehler correlation (r_HM),
    the latent copula correlation, and the shape-attenuation factor (A), which
    quantifies the ceiling on manifest association imposed by marginal shape
    mismatch (Hoeffding-Frechet bounds); (2) The distribution-free effect size
    (d_reg) for independent and paired samples, retaining the interpretation
    and metric of Cohen's d / Hedges' g while drastically reducing sampling
    variance and mean squared error (MSE) in small samples (n < 50); and (3)
    Analytical and bootstrap confidence intervals alongside diagnostic
    visualization tools.
